--TEST--
A `Core\Heap` comparator that pops the heap it is ordering makes the outer `pop` throw a catchable `RuntimeError` once a position it compares is gone, and the heap is still usable afterwards — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 9
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Heap::pop, Core\Heap::isEmpty
final class Drain {
    public static ?Core\Heap<int> $heap = null;

    public static function empties(int $a, int $b): int {
        var $heap = Drain::$heap;
        if ($heap != null) {
            if (!$heap->isEmpty()) {
                $heap->pop();
            }
        }
        return $a - $b;
    }
}

var $h = new Core\Heap<int>(Drain::empties(...));
int $i = 0;
while ($i < 8) {
    $h->push(8 - $i);
    $i = $i + 1;
}

// Every comparison takes one more element out, so the sift that follows a
// pop runs out of positions to compare. The request goes on.
Drain::$heap = $h;
try {
    while (!$h->isEmpty()) {
        $h->pop();
    }
    echo "drained without a throw\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
Drain::$heap = null;

// Whatever is left can still be counted, emptied and reused.
while (!$h->isEmpty()) {
    $h->pop();
}
$h->push(3);
echo $h->count(), " ", $h->peek(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Heap::pop() lost positions it was ordering: the comparator took elements out of the heap it compares
1 3
